To specify a call to the constructor of the base class when writing the definition of a constructor of the derived class, you can use the base class constructor's name followed by the parameters in parentheses. This is typically done in the initializer list of the derived class constructor.

For example, if you have a base class called `rectangleType` and a derived class called `boxType`, you can specify a call to the base class constructor like this:

```cpp
boxType::boxType(double l, double w, double h) 
    : rectangleType(l, w), height(h) {
    // constructor body
}
```

In this example, `rectangleType(l, w)` calls the constructor of the base class `rectangleType` with parameters `l` and `w`. The `height(h)` initializes the `height` member variable of the derived class.

